      "text": "Rain is one of the most fundamental and vital phenomena on Earth, serving as the primary mechanism for distributing fresh water across the planet\u2019s surface. While it may appear to be a simple event\u2014water falling from the sky\u2014the process of rain formation is a complex, multi-stage journey driven by solar energy, atmospheric physics, and thermodynamics. To truly understand how rain forms, one must examine the complete water cycle, also known as the hydrologic cycle, which describes the continuous movement of water on, above, and below the surface of the Earth. This cycle is not a linear path but a dynamic, interconnected system involving evaporation, condensation, precipitation, and collection.\n\nThe story of rain begins long before a single drop falls from a cloud. It starts with the sun, the engine that powers the entire water cycle. The sun\u2019s energy heats the Earth\u2019s surface, particularly the vast oceans, which cover approximately 71% of the planet. This solar radiation provides the thermal energy necessary to change the state of water from liquid to gas, a process known as evaporation. During evaporation, water molecules at the surface of oceans, lakes, and rivers gain enough kinetic energy to break free from the liquid phase and enter the atmosphere as water vapor. This invisible gas rises",
